  "body": "## What changed\n\nThe index-swing clause of the program gate (#804, #813) judged only an A that turns the work about X. A table **B** (stated `[0,1,0]`, or unstated and named B) and an **A stated level but off X** read as \"off X\", so they got no swing check, no refusal and no load note. A `G0 B..` index could swing the work into the retracted tool without anything judging it. This PR generalises the clause to any work-turning rotary about a **horizontal** line through the WCS origin.\n\n- `crates/app/src/posting_index_swing.rs`\n  - `rotary_line` now returns the line itself:\n    - `Horizontal(u)`: a stated level vector, normalised, or an unstated `A` (X) or `B` (Y). Judged.\n    - `Vertical`: a stated vector along ±Z, or an unstated `C`. It keeps every height, so it stays safe and unjudged, as before.\n    - `Leaning`: a stated vector that is neither level nor vertical, or a zero or non-finite one. **Refused by name** (\"about a line that is neither level nor vertical ... its index swing is not judged\"). It is never judged as if it were level.\n    - `Unknown`: no vector and no RS-274 letter. Refused, as in #813, and this refusal still wins over the others.\n  - \"Level\" is now strict: the off component must be within 1e-6 of the vector's length. That is 1 µm of height over 1 m of travel. The old X test allowed about 5.7° of lean.\n  - The swept radius is the farthest turning vertex from the rotary's own line, `|(p - o) × u|`. For u = X this is the old `hypot(y, z)`.\n  - An unstated `nose_to_axis` refuses by name, exactly as for A. A non-finite one refuses by name too. `LoadMachine` already refuses a non-finite number at creation, so this is defence in depth.\n  - A non-finite facet vertex now counts as an unbounded sweep, so the post is refused. Before, that vertex was skipped.\n  - **Two rotaries turning the work** (a table B carrying a C, a trunnion): each turn moves the other's line, so the sweep is taken from the WCS origin itself, and the message says so. No shipped machine has this setup.\n  - The index angle in the messages is read about the rotary's line, `atan2(t·(u×Z), t·Z)`. That is the post's `atan2(-y, z)` for X and `atan2(x, z)` for Y.\n- `crates/app/src/project/swing_note.rs`: the pre-#804 load note now names every rotary with an unstated `turns_part` unless its line is vertical. A saved table B is now named on load.\n- Decision record: `docs/decisions/818-swing-any-horizontal.md`. It covers why a leaning line refuses instead of getting a \"conservative\" judgement, the 1e-6 level tolerance, and the stacked-rotary sweep from the origin.\n- Shipped machines are unchanged. The only machine that turns the work on a level line is the microARC (A, `[1,0,0]`), and it is judged exactly as before.\n\n## The math\n\nA vertical tool's tip at height h above a horizontal line through the origin is at distance `sqrt(h² + d²) ≥ h` from it, where d is its level offset from the line. So the tip is the tool's nearest point to the line wherever the G30 puts it along X and Y. The #804 law, `tip = nose_to_axis - tool length ≥ swept + MARGIN_MM`, therefore holds unchanged for any level unit vector u; only the swept radius `max |(p - o) × u|` depends on u.\n\nA leaning line rises along its travel, so its distance to the tip depends on the unknown G30 position. No bound short of the whole envelope holds, which is why it is refused. Turns about lines through the origin keep each point's distance from the origin, so that distance is the conservative sweep when two rotaries turn the work.\n\n## Tests\n\nNew suite `crates/app/tests/index_swing_horizontal.rs` (10 tests). It uses a test-only table-B machine file, inline as `TABLE_B`: a 770MX-sized mill with a work-turning B `[0,1,0]` and `nose_to_axis` 10.6 in.\n\n- `a_table_b_too_long_to_clear_the_bar_is_refused_by_name`: B90 index. The bar's far end sweeps `hypot(STICK, R)` about Y. The test checks the refusal line, the 3.000 mm clearance and that the post writes nothing.\n- `a_table_b_with_a_short_tool_clears`: no false refusal.\n- `a_table_b_with_no_nose_height_is_refused_by_name`\n- `a_table_b_with_no_axis_vector_is_judged_about_y`: an unstated B is judged about Y.\n- `an_a_stated_level_but_off_x_is_judged_about_its_own_line`: A `[3,4,0]`, swept about `[0.6,0.8,0]`. It refuses and clears at the same lengths as the A case.\n- `a_leaning_or_zero_work_turning_line_is_refused_not_judged`: `[1,0,1]` and `[0,0,0]` are refused by name, and the post writes nothing.\n- `a_non_finite_vector_or_nose_height_never_reads_as_judged`: `LoadMachine` refuses NaN and inf. `rotary_line` reads NaN or inf as `Leaning`, `[0,2,0]` as unit Y, `[0,1,0.01]` as `Leaning` and `[0,0,-3]` as `Vertical`.\n- `a_work_turning_c_alone_stays_unjudged`: stated and unstated C, a too-long tool and no nose height. Nothing is raised.\n- `two_rotaries_turning_the_work_sweep_from_the_wcs_origin`: B with C. The sweep is the bar corner's distance from the origin.\n- `a_project_saved_before_804_names_its_unjudged_table_b_on_load`\n\nThe existing `index_swing` suite (9) passes unchanged.\n\n## Revert check\n\nI restored `posting_index_swing.rs` and `swing_note.rs` to `upstream/main`, ran the new suite, then restored the fix.\n\n- **Fail on revert:** the B refusal, the no-nose refusal, the unstated-B judgement, the off-X A, the leaning/zero refusal, the stacked sweep and the load note (7 tests).\n- **Does not compile on revert:** `a_non_finite_vector_or_nose_height_never_reads_as_judged`. It uses the new `RotaryLine` variants, so for this check I set it aside temporarily.\n- **Pass on revert, as intended:** `a_table_b_with_a_short_tool_clears` and `a_work_turning_c_alone_stays_unjudged`. They guard against false refusals and are not pins of the fix.\n- **Separate stacked check:** I forced `stacked: false` and the fixed code passed everything except `two_rotaries_turning_the_work_sweep_from_the_wcs_origin`, so the origin sweep is pinned on its own.\n\n## Gate\n\nAll results are on Linux, on head `0722074d`.\n\n- **`cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/main`:** green. The app suites step ran 62 suites mapped to `app::project` plus the lib, in 211 s, followed by the 10 new tests. 0 failed.\n- **Pinning tests:** run through `cargo xtask gate test` (`--crate app --binary index_swing_horizontal`). Green.\n- **Existing swing and rotary suites:**\n\n| Suite | Result |\n|---|---|\n| index_swing | 9 passed |\n| tormach_fourth_axis | 12 passed |\n| microarc_test_part | 2 passed |\n| rotary_letter | 3 passed |\n| rotary_verify | 9 passed |\n| conformance | 6 passed |\n| posting | 17 passed |\n| machine | 33 passed |\n\n- **`cargo clippy -p camcraft-app --all-targets -- -D warnings`:** clean.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- I never posted a table-B program that clears: the PathPilot post is the only post in the fixture, and the test checks only the gate, not B output.\n- No shipped machine turns the work on B or on two rotaries, so none was exercised; the stacked case runs only on the test machine.\n- The headless-load note gap (review note 2 on #815) is pre-existing and unchanged.\n\nChangelog: The index-swing check now judges any rotary that turns the work about a level line (a table B, an A stated off X), not only an A about X. It refuses by name a leaning or garbled rotary line, and with two work-turning rotaries it takes the sweep from the WCS origin (#818)\n\nEffort: high (no escalation)\n\nFork synced at v0.17.0.\n\nFixes #818\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
